      "Linear perspective is a geometric method for showing three-dimensional space on a flat surface. In Florence in the early fifteenth century, the architect Filippo Brunelleschi demonstrated it, around 1415 to 1420, with a painted panel of the Baptistery that a viewer checked against the real building through a peephole and a mirror. In 1435 Leon Battista Alberti set the method out in writing in his treatise On Painting, which compared a picture to an open window through which the viewer looks at the scene.",
      "In one-point perspective, lines that run straight away from the viewer, called orthogonals, are drawn so that they converge at a single vanishing point. The vanishing point lies on the horizon line, which sits at the viewer's eye level. Objects of the same real size are drawn smaller the farther away they are, at a rate the construction fixes, so the whole picture is seen from one fixed viewpoint.",
      "Masaccio's Holy Trinity fresco in Santa Maria Novella, painted around 1427, is one of the earliest paintings built on this system: its coffered barrel vault appears to recede into the wall. In Leonardo da Vinci's Last Supper, painted in the 1490s, the orthogonals of the room converge at Christ's head, so the geometry also directs the viewer's attention to the central figure.",
      "Painters also used atmospheric, or aerial, perspective: distant things appear paler, bluer and less sharply detailed because of the air between them and the viewer. Leonardo described and used this effect. Medieval painting had often sized figures by importance rather than by distance, a convention called hierarchical scale; it served religious meaning rather than being a failed attempt at realism."
