      "Mixing light and mixing paint follow different rules. Light mixes additively: the primaries red, green and blue combine toward white, which is how screens make color. Pigments mix subtractively, because each absorbs some wavelengths and reflects the rest: the printing primaries cyan, magenta and yellow combine toward a dark, near-black color. The traditional painter's wheel uses red, yellow and blue as primaries, with orange, green and violet as secondaries.",
      "Complementary colors lie opposite each other on the color wheel, such as red and green, blue and orange, or yellow and violet. Placed side by side, complements make each other look more intense; mixed together, they neutralize toward grey or brown. Painters often darken or dull a color by adding a little of its complement instead of black.",
      "Colors are also described as warm, such as reds, oranges and yellows, or cool, such as blues, greens and violets; warm colors tend to seem to advance and cool colors to recede. Strong contrast of value or of hue draws the eye, so artists use it to create emphasis, while a limited, related palette builds unity."
