      "Tonal music is organized around a central note, the tonic, which sounds like home. A major scale climbs from the tonic in a fixed pattern of whole steps and half steps: whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half. Starting on C, it uses only the white keys of the piano: C, D, E, F, G, A, B, C. The scale degrees are numbered from 1 to 7, and the seventh degree, a half step below the tonic, is called the leading tone because it pulls upward to the tonic.",
      "A triad is a chord of three notes stacked in thirds: a root, a third and a fifth. Building a triad on each degree of the major scale gives major triads on degrees 1, 4 and 5, minor triads on 2, 3 and 6, and a diminished triad on 7. Roman numerals name these chords, uppercase for major and lowercase for minor: in C major, I is C-E-G, IV is F-A-C and V is G-B-D.",
      "The dominant chord, V, contains the leading tone, so it creates tension that resolves to the tonic chord, I. Adding a seventh above the root gives the dominant seventh chord, V7; in C major it is G-B-D-F, and the tritone between B and F intensifies the pull, B rising to C and F falling to E.",
      "A cadence is a harmonic ending to a phrase. An authentic cadence moves V to I and sounds final. A plagal cadence moves IV to I, the familiar Amen ending of hymns. A half cadence ends on V and sounds unfinished, like a comma. A deceptive cadence moves V to vi, surprising the listener by avoiding the expected tonic."
