      "The beat is the steady pulse a listener taps along to. Tempo is the speed of the beat, measured in beats per minute or described with Italian terms such as largo for very slow, andante for a walking pace, allegro for fast and presto for very fast. Rhythm is the pattern of long and short durations that sounds against the beat.",
      "Meter groups beats into recurring patterns of strong and weak beats, and each group is written as a measure, or bar. Duple meter has two beats per measure, triple meter three and quadruple meter four. The first beat of a measure, the downbeat, is usually the strongest. A waltz is in triple meter: strong, weak, weak.",
      "A time signature shows the meter. In simple meters each beat divides into two equal parts: 2/4, 3/4 and 4/4 have two, three and four quarter-note beats. In compound meters each beat divides into three: 6/8 has two beats per measure, each a dotted quarter note divided into three eighth notes. So 6/8 and 3/4 both contain six eighth notes per measure but are felt differently, as two groups of three versus three groups of two.",
      "Syncopation places accents where the meter does not expect them, on weak beats or between beats, creating rhythmic surprise. It is central to ragtime, jazz and much popular music. Tempo can also change on purpose: accelerando means gradually faster and ritardando gradually slower."
