      "Phonetics studies the physical sounds of speech: how they are produced, transmitted and heard. Because ordinary spelling is an unreliable guide to sound, linguists use the International Phonetic Alphabet, or IPA, in which each symbol stands for one sound. English spelling, for example, writes the same vowel sound in see, sea and ceiling with different letters.",
      "Consonants are described by three features. Voicing tells whether the vocal folds vibrate: [b] and [z] are voiced, [p] and [s] are voiceless. Place of articulation tells where the airflow is obstructed: at the two lips for bilabial [p], [b] and [m], at the ridge behind the upper teeth for alveolar [t], [d] and [n], and at the soft palate for velar [k] and [g]. Manner of articulation tells how: stops block the air completely, fricatives narrow it to make friction, as in [f] and [s], and nasals send it through the nose.",
      "Vowels are described by tongue height, from high to low, by tongue position from front to back, and by lip rounding. The vowel [i] in beet is high, front and unrounded; [u] in boot is high, back and rounded; [a] is low.",
      "Phonology studies how a particular language organizes sounds into a system. A phoneme is a sound unit that distinguishes words in that language, shown by minimal pairs such as pat and bat, which differ in one sound. Allophones are predictable variants of one phoneme: English /p/ is aspirated, with a puff of air, at the start of pin, but unaspirated in spin, and English speakers hear both as the same sound. Languages draw these lines differently: in Korean, the aspirated and plain stops written ㅍ and ㅂ are separate phonemes that distinguish words."
