    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about the parts that words are made of. Explain the ideas and give examples, in your own words. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Morphology studies the internal structure of words. A morpheme is the smallest unit of language that carries meaning or grammatical function. The word unhappiness has three morphemes: un-, meaning not, the root happy, and -ness, which makes a noun. A morpheme is not the same as a syllable: the word elephant has three syllables but only one morpheme.",
      "Free morphemes can stand alone as words, such as happy, cat or run. Bound morphemes must attach to another morpheme, such as the prefix un- or the suffixes -ness and -s. Prefixes attach before a root and suffixes after it.",
      "Inflection adds grammatical information such as number, tense or person without creating a new word or changing its category: cat and cats are forms of one noun, and walk and walked are forms of one verb. Derivation creates a new word, often of a different category: happy is an adjective, happiness a noun, and teach becomes teacher. Compounding joins two roots into one word, as in blackboard or toothbrush; a blackboard need not be black, which shows that a compound's meaning is not simply the sum of its parts.",
      "Languages differ in how they build words. Isolating languages such as Mandarin and Vietnamese use mostly single-morpheme words and express grammar with word order and separate words. Agglutinative languages such as Turkish and Korean stack morphemes in sequence, each usually carrying one meaning; Korean attaches particles and endings to stems, as in 먹었습니다, built from the stem 먹- eat, the past marker -었-, and the formal ending -습니다. Fusional languages such as Spanish and Latin pack several meanings into one ending: the -o in Spanish hablo marks first person, singular, present tense and indicative mood at once."
    ],
