  {
    "title": "Writing Systems and Hangul",
    "category": "arts-languages",
    "subject": "Linguistics",
    "topic": "Writing systems",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about the kinds of writing systems people use and how Hangul was designed. Explain the ideas and give examples, in your own words. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Writing represents spoken language with visible marks. It was invented independently several times: in Mesopotamia, where cuneiform developed around 3200 BCE, in China, and in Mesoamerica. Speech is far older; most of the world's languages have been spoken without being written.",
      "Writing systems differ in what unit each symbol stands for. In a logographic system, a symbol represents a word or morpheme; Chinese characters work this way, and most of them include a component that hints at pronunciation, so they are not pure pictures of ideas. In a syllabary, each symbol stands for a syllable, as in Japanese kana or the Cherokee script. In an alphabet, symbols stand for individual consonants and vowels, as in Greek, Latin and Cyrillic. An abjad writes mainly consonants and leaves most vowels to the reader, as in Arabic and Hebrew. An abugida writes consonants with a built-in vowel that marks change, as in Devanagari, used for Hindi. Japanese combines kanji, which are logographic, with kana.",
      "Hangul, the Korean alphabet, is unusual because it was deliberately designed and its origin is documented. It was created under King Sejong the Great in 1443 and promulgated in 1446 in a document called Hunminjeongeum, The Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People. Before it, Koreans wrote with Chinese characters, which were hard for ordinary people to learn and fit the Korean language poorly. South Korea celebrates Hangul Day on October 9.",
      "Hangul is often called a featural alphabet. The basic consonant shapes depict the speech organs that make them: ㄱ shows the back of the tongue raised toward the soft palate, ㄴ the tongue tip touching the ridge behind the teeth, ㅁ the closed mouth, ㅅ a tooth and ㅇ the throat. Related sounds add strokes, so aspirated ㅋ adds a stroke to ㄱ. The vowels were built from three symbols for heaven, a dot, earth, a horizontal line, and human, a vertical line. Letters are grouped into syllable blocks: 한 is ㅎ plus ㅏ plus ㄴ. Modern Hangul has 14 basic consonants and 10 basic vowels. Because each letter is a consonant or vowel, Hangul is an alphabet arranged in syllables, not a syllabary."
    ],
